[{"page":1,"text":"NOTICE OF A SPECIAL\n\nCITY COUNCIL MEETING\n\nMarch 31, 2026, at 6:00 PM\n\nPresent\n\nMayor Zack Stratton\n\nCouncilmember Parker McCumber\n\nCouncilmember Jacob Wood\n\nCouncilmember Jacob Holdaway\n\nCouncilmember David Lauret\n\nCouncilmember Ezra Nair\n\nAbsent\n\nStaff Present: Chief Deputy Holden Rockwell with the Utah County Sherrif’s Office,\nAdministrative Director David Kyle Herring, Building Director Cris Thomas, Public Works\nDirector Naseem Ghandour, City Recorder Robin Bond, and Deputy City Recorder Tony Lara\nOthers Speaking: Dave and Cam Terry with EZ Park. Vineyard residents Daria Evans, Kim Cornelius,\n\nChip Price, David Pearce, Krystal Price, Jacob Schooley, and Steven Moss\n\n1.\n\nCALL TO ORDER\n\nMayor Stratton called the meeting to order at 6:00pm.\n\n2.\n\nINVOCATION OR INSPIRATIONAL THOUGHT (BY INVITATION)\n\n3.\n\nPLEDGE OF ALLEGIANCE (BY INVITATION)\n\n4.\n\nPUBLIC HEARING\n\n4.1. Amend the Consolidated Fee Schedule for the Transportation Utility Fee and\n\nVineyard Days. Allow the Mayor the power to lower Fees on the Consolidated Fee\n\nSchedule\n\nAdministrative Director David Kyle Herring outlined the changes to the Consolidated Fee\nSchedule which included setting the Transportation Utility Fee (TUF) to $0.00 as well as\nchanges to fees associated with Vineyard Days vendors and participants.\n\nDaria Evans, living in the Villas neighborhood, questioned removing the fee for businesses and\nresidents. She asked how many businesses were affected.\nCouncilmember McCumber replied with the business impact. He felt the fee was unwelcoming\nPage 1 of 5; March 31, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"to businesses. He mentioned that the Megaplex theater, but also smaller businesses had been\nadversely affected. He felt spending needed to be cut.\nKim Cornelius, living in the Villas neighborhood, questioned if deleting the impact fee\nwould be a better solution than simply setting the cost to 0.\nCouncilmember Nair commented that he did not vote for the Transportation Utility Fee. He\nfelt the resident side was equitable, but the business side seemed excessive. He would like to see\nthe fee restructured. His main concerns were road maintenance, and possible delays in routine\nmaintenance. He felt rebuilding a road would be more costly. He wished to avoid having to do\nnew studies required by the state should the council wish to reinstate the fee later.\nThe council briefly discussed the possibility of including a form of the transportation utility\nfee as part of an initial business license fee.\nChip Price, living in the Providence neighborhood, expressed concern with the fee and felt\nthat the city had already achieved the maximum amount of taxation that it could and supported\nremoving the TUF.\nMr. Herring explained the reasoning behind the portion of the resolution that allowed the\nmayor to reduce fees. The council briefly discussed the merits of that change, with some on\ncouncil expressing concern that it may result in an influx of requests to the mayor’s office for\nlower fees.\n\n5.\n\nMAYOR AND COUNCILMEMBERS' DISCLOSURES/RECUSALS\n\nCouncilmember Holdaway spoke about the proposed work to occur on 400 South near his\nproperty. He stated that it was purchased after the right of way had been established and that he\ndoes not financially benefit from the property and does not believe there is a conflict of interest.\nHe added that in regards to his father’s property that if it came to an eminent domain issue that\nhe would not participate in any vote for that.\n\n6.\n\nPUBLIC COMMENT\n\nDaria Evan, living in The Villas neighborhood, thanked the community for Library\n\nDonations.\nDavid Pearce, living in the Cascade neighborhood, also thanked the community for the\ndonations. He also wanted to state that he agreed with Councilmember Lauret, he would be\nconcerned if the council gave its authority away with respect to fees and taxes, as he felt that was\nakin to removing the voice of the tax payer.\nKrystal Price, living in the Waters Edge neighborhood, asked the council about the status of\ntransferring the HOA from the developers to the neighborhood. The council discussed working\nwith legal to find an answer to the issue.\nPage 2 of 5; March 31, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"7.\n\nWORK SESSION\n\n7.1. EZ Park Presentation and Discussion\n\nDave and Cam Terry, with EZ Park, presented their parking enforcement program and\nhighlighted its success in other cities. They emphasized an approach that avoids towing, noting\nthat towing often creates conflict, and instead demonstrated an automated camera system that\nreads license plates and issues digital citations. The program also includes digital permitting,\nwith reporting provided directly to the city, and is designed to be scalable as a comprehensive\nparking management solution. They noted that signage would be installed by EZ Park to inform\nresidents, with an estimated implementation timeline of 30 days.\nThe Council discussed parking enforcement operations, including citation costs, permitting,\nand towing procedures. Councilmember Wood asked about patrol practices and costs.\nCouncilmember Wood questioned permit pricing and the number of vehicles allowed per permit,\nit was clarified that the Council could establish those limits. Councilmember Holdaway\nrequested that enforcement information be directed to the Code Enforcement Officer.\nJacob Schooley, living in the Lake Front neighborhood asked about the customization of the\nescalation policy as well as for clarification on the monthly fee for a pass. A discussion ensued.\nSteven Moss, living in the Bridgeport neighborhood, asked for clarification on data storage\nand where specifically the license plate data would be stored, expressing data privacy concerns.\n\n8.\n\nBUSINESS ITEMS\n\n8.1. Resolution 2026-21 Amend the Consolidated Fee Schedule\n\nThe mayor and council discussed the proposed amendments as well as the proposed change\nallowing for the mayor to lower fees if needed. Councilmember McCumber asked that the\ncouncil retain the power to set fees. Both he and Councilmember Nair felt it was important to\nremove that section from the resolutions. As to the removal of the Transportation Utility Fee, the\ncouncil felt it was a good idea and were confident that the budget would be adequate for road\nmaintenance.\n\nMr. Pearce agreed with Councilmember McCumbers thoughts on the council retaining\ncontrol. He stated that the frequency of council meetings would allow for a quick response to\nsomeone seeking a reduction in fees.\n\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER LAURET MOVED TO ADOPT RESOLUTION 2026-21,\n\nWITH THE PROPOSED CHANGES TO SECTION 5 AS PRESENTED. COUNCILMEMBER\n\nHOLDAWAY SECONDED. THE ROLL CALL WAS AS FOLLOWS: COUNCILMEMBERS\n\nNAIR, LAURET, WOOD, HOLDAWAY, AND MCCUMBER VOTED IN FAVOR. THE\n\nMOTION PASSED UNANIMOUSLY.\n\n8.2. Adopt Resolution 2026-19 In Support Of Parking Enforcement\nPage 3 of 5; March 31, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"The Council considered a resolution supporting increased parking enforcement, focusing on\naddressing immediate safety hazards in high-density neighborhoods such as Bridgeport and\nLeCheminant. Discussion centered on whether a formal resolution was necessary versus\nadministrative action, as well as balancing enforcement priorities with other public safety\ndemands. Chief Deputy Rockwell outlined current enforcement efforts, operational constraints,\nand recommended amendments to the resolution to allow for officer discretion, use of authorized\ntowing providers, and flexibility in responding to higher-priority calls.\nMs. Evans suggested some minor text edits to both resolution 2026-19 as well as to the\ndocuments that were part of the agenda packet for Operation Neighborhood Shield.\n\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER MCCUMBER MOTIONED TO ADOPT RESOLUTION\n\n2026-19 IN SUPPORT OF PARKING ENFORCEMENT AS AMENDED.\n\nCOUNCILMEMBER NAIR SECONDED. THE ROLLCALL WAS AS FOLLOWS\n\nCOUNCILMEMBERS NAIR, LAURET, WOOD, HOLDAWAY, AND MCCUMBER VOTED\n\nIN FAVOR. THE MOTION PASSED UNANIMOUSLY.\n\n8.3. Adopt Resolution 2026-20 To Form A Community Events Non-Profit\n\nThe Council discussed a resolution to establish a community events commission and\nfacilitate the creation of a nonprofit organization to support city events and encourage\nvolunteerism. The intent was to shift events toward a more community-driven model, with the\nnonprofit enabling fundraising and tax-deductible donations. Councilmembers asked clarifying\nquestions about the relationship between the commission and the nonprofit, governance\nstructure, and legal considerations, including oversight, leadership roles, and the process for\ndrafting and approving a charter.\nCity Attorney Jesse Riddle advised that the resolution primarily serves as a starting point,\nwith additional structure and policies to be developed later. The Council debated language to\nensure appropriate oversight and clarity, ultimately amending the proposal to authorize the\nmayor to lead the nonprofit’s creation, require council approval of appointments and the\nnonprofit charter, and allow the commission to elect its own leadership.\nMr. Pearce asked for clarification on the process of having the mayor appoint the chair of the\ncommission and not having the commission itself vote later to appoint the chair from their\nmembership.\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER MCCUMBER MOVED TO ADOPT RESOLUTION 202620 TO FORM A COMMUNITY EVENTS NON-PROFIT, WITH THE AMENDMENTS\n\nAGREED UPON DURING THE MEETING. COUNCILMEMBER LAURET SECONDED.\n\nTHE ROLL CALL WAS AS FOLLOWS: COUNCILMEMBERS NAIR, LAURET, WOOD,\n\nHOLDAWAY, AND MCCUMBER VOTED IN FAVOR. THE MOTION PASSED\n\nUNANIMOUSLY.\n\n8.4. Approve the Neighborhood Shield Program\n\nThis item was removed from the agenda to be addressed at a future meeting.\n\n8.5. Approve Creating A Neighborhood Commission, Establishing Neighborhood\n\nDistricts, And Providing For The Appointment and Selection Of Commission\n\nMembers\nPage 4 of 5; March 31, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"The Council discussed a proposed neighborhood commission (Item 8.5), with initial\nsuggestions to move the item to a future work session for further refinement. Discussion\nhighlighted the value of recent neighborhood-led collaboration efforts, where residents\norganically organized, selected leaders, and built consensus without formal city intervention.\nSeveral councilmembers expressed concern that creating a formal commission could introduce\nunnecessary bureaucracy and lack community buy-in, noting that residents would likely continue\nto engage directly with the Council regardless. Alternative approaches were discussed, including\nrelying on existing processes such as traffic calming petitions to ensure representative input.\n\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER LAURET MOVED TO CONTINUE THE ITEM TO THE\n\nNEXT WORK SESSION. COUNCILMEMBER WOOD SECONDED. THE COUNCIL\n\nDISCUSSED THE MOTION.\n\nMotion: COUNCILMEMBER NAIR COUNTERMOTIONED TO STRIKE THE ITEM\n\nFROM FUTURE AGENDAS. COUNCILMEMBER MCCUMBER SECONDED. THE ROLL\n\nCALL WAS AS FOLLOWS: COUNCILMEMBERS NAIR, LAURET, WOOD, HOLDAWAY,\n\nAND MCCUMBER VOTED IN FAVOR. THE MOTION PASSED UNANIMOUSLY.\n\n9.\n\nADJOURNMENT\n\nThe meeting adjourned at 8:15 pm.\n\nMINUTES APPROVED ON:\n\n5/5/2026\n\nCERTIFIED CORRECT BY:\n\nTONY LARA, DEPUTY CITY RECORDER\n\nPage 5 of 5; March 31, 2026, City Council Agenda","ocr":false}]